WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 73: The Voodoo Queen of New Orleans: Marie Laveau and the Mysteries of Congo Square

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.760
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:20.320
[Rebecca] In 19th century New Orleans, amidst the opulent gaslit mansions of the French Quarter and the Spanish-tiled courtyards of the Creole elite, one woman held absolute sway over the spiritual and social imagination of the Crescent City.

4
00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:25.680
[Rebecca] She was not a wealthy white sugar planter, a Spanish grandee, or a military general.

5
00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:30.240
[Rebecca] She was a free woman of color, born out of wedlock, Marie Levo.

6
00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.680
[Rebecca] Universally crowned the Voodoo Queen of New Orleans.

7
00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:48.720
[Rebecca] Marie was a master herbalist, an astute political strategist, a devoted Catholic philanthropist, and a formidable spiritual leader, whose influence reached from the cabins of enslaved dockworkers straight into the parlor rooms of judges and governors.

8
00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:55.600
[Rebecca] George brings us the true, enchanting history of Marie Levo.

9
00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:02.640
[George] In the early 19th century, New Orleans possessed a social and racial hierarchy unique in North America.

10
00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:18.000
[George] Under the French and Spanish colonial legal code known as the Code Noir, there existed a distinct, legally recognized intermediate class between enslaved Africans and white planters, the gens de couleurs libress, or free people of color.

11
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:27.440
[George] Often educated, bilingual, and property-owning, free people of color worked as master iron workers, merchants, artists, and herbalists.

12
00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:39.520
[George] Into this complex Creole society was born Marie Catherine Levaux in September 1801, the daughter of a free Creole woman of color and Charles Levo, a prosperous mulatto merchant.

13
00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:51.520
[George] Raised in the vibrant French quarter, Marie was a striking, charismatic woman with dark flowing hair, piercing golden brown eyes, and an imposing presence that commanded immediate respect.

14
00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:59.120
[George] In 1819, Marie married Jacques Paris, a free carpenter from Saint Domingue who had fled the Haitian Revolution.

15
00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:14.120
[George] When Paris mysteriously vanished several years later, Marie adopted the legal title of Widow Paris, later entering a lifelong common law union with Christophy Glappian, a French nobleman's descendant, with whom she had fifteen children.

16
00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:30.760
[George] New Orleans was a unique racial melting pot governed by the French Code Noir and Spanish civil law, where a prosperous class of free people of color owned property, operated businesses, and practiced Catholic devotions alongside ancient West African spiritual traditions.

17
00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:45.160
[George] The vibrant French quarter of early 19th century New Orleans was a world of shaded courtyards, wrought iron balconies, and diverse languages, where French, Spanish, English, and African dialects mingled in bustling markets.

18
00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:55.720
[George] To support her growing family in the 1820s, Marie established herself as an elite professional hairdresser to the wealthiest white Creole aristocrats in the French quarter.

19
00:02:55.720 --> 00:03:08.120
[George] In an era of elaborate Victorian coiffeures featuring towering curls, fragrant pomades and pearl pins, a skilled hairdresser was an intimate, indispensable visitor in aristocratic boudoirs.

20
00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:18.440
[George] As Marie brushed and styled the hair of prominent judges' wives, senators' daughters, and wealthy merchant matrons, she listened with keen, silent intelligence.

21
00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:30.840
[George] Behind closed doors, white elite women confessed their deepest, darkest family secrets, illicit affairs, hidden debts, disputed wills, domestic abuse, and political bribery.

22
00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:40.520
[George] At the same time, Marie built an extensive, loyal network of African American domestic servants, cooks, and carriage drivers across the city.

23
00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:54.320
[George] By cross-referencing information whispered in boudoirs with gossip overheard in servant quarters, Marie constructed an unrivaled private intelligence network that made her the most informed individual in the entire state of Louisiana.

24
00:03:54.320 --> 00:04:12.080
[George] In the opulent dressing rooms of wealthy French and Spanish matriarchs, Marie styled elaborate wigs while listening attentively to family secrets, business bankruptcies, political alliances, and marital infidelities that gave her unmatched psychological leverage across the crescent city.

25
00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:25.360
[George] Marie's keen emotional intelligence and reputation for absolute discretion made her a trusted confidant to women of every social rank, allowing her to dispense astute life advice alongside fashionable hairstyles.

26
00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:37.920
[George] While popular mythology frequently portrays voodoo as a sinister, cinematic realm of black magic and curses, Louisiana voodoo was in reality a sophisticated, legitimate syncretic religion.

27
00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:56.800
[George] Transported to the Mississippi Delta by enslaved West Africans from Dahomey and the Congo, and reinforced by thousands of Haitian refugees following the revolution of 1804, Voodoo centered upon deep reverence for ancestors, natural elemental spirits known as Lois, and a supreme creator god.

28
00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:03.920
[George] Marie Lévau's genius lay in her brilliant synthesis of West African animism with Orthodox Roman Catholicism.

29
00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:06.400
[George] A lifelong, daily communicant at St.

30
00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:12.080
[George] Louis Cathedral on Jackson Square, Marie saw no contradiction between the two traditions.

31
00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:13.200
[George] Her home on St.

32
00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:18.000
[George] Anne Street was adorned with magnificent Catholic altars featuring statues of St.

33
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:27.440
[George] Peter, Saint Michael, and the Virgin Mary, surrounded by blessed candles, holy water, sea shells, and sacred African Grigory bags.

34
00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:32.600
[George] For Marie, Catholic saints were direct spiritual manifestations of African loas.

35
00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:37.720
[George] Saint Patrick represented Dambala, the great serpent spirit of wisdom.

36
00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:41.560
[George] Saint Anthony was Legba, the guardian of the crossroads.

37
00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:49.560
[George] This spiritual fusion allowed her to minister to Catholics of all races without attracting persecution from the Catholic archdiocese.

38
00:05:49.560 --> 00:06:07.080
[George] Marie's personal altar blended wooden crucifixes, statues of Saint Patrick and the Virgin Mary, and holy water fonts with sacred West African Grigory charms, snake bones and herbal potions, creating a profound syncretic spiritual practice that welcomed all races.

39
00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:21.160
[George] Practitioners of Louisiana voodoo recognized Marie as a profound spiritual matriarch whose rituals honored ancestral spirits, natural forces, and Christian saints in an unbroken circle of sacred healing and protection.

40
00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:26.840
[George] Under Louisiana law, enslaved workers were granted Sunday afternoons off from labor.

41
00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:34.760
[George] In New Orleans, these Sunday hours were spent at a dusty public commons just outside the city ramparts known as Congo Square.

42
00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:36.280
[George] By the hundreds.

43
00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:54.360
[George] Enslaved and free Africans gathered in tribal circles, Yoruba, Mandinka, Ibo and Congolese, singing in native dialects, dancing to the polyrhythmic thrum of hollowed log drums, banjos and gourd rattles, and bartering homegrown vegetables, pralines and herbal remedies.

44
00:06:54.360 --> 00:07:03.240
[George] It was the only place in North America where African music, dance, and cultural identity survived openly in pure, collective form.

45
00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:06.440
[George] Presiding over these gatherings was Marie Lavo.

46
00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:18.480
[George] Dressed in her signature flowing Creole Tignon, a vibrant madras handkerchief tied high on her head with seven points, which state sumptuary laws had originally intended as a mark of subservience.

47
00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:24.400
[George] But which Marie transformed into a regal crown, she moved through the crowds like an empress.

48
00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:34.560
[George] Congo Square was not only the cultural fountainhead that would give birth to American jazz, but the public stage where Marie cemented her undisputed status as the Queen of Voodoo.

49
00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:51.360
[George] Hundreds of enslaved people and free creoles gathered in the dust of Congo Square each Sunday afternoon, singing haunting call and response chants and dancing the bambula to the hypnotic rhythm of hollow log drums, preserving African musical heritage that birthed American jazz.

50
00:07:51.360 --> 00:08:04.080
[George] Municipal authorities were wary of the large weekly gatherings at Congo Square, deploying armed guards to monitor the perimeter, yet the power of the drum circles and communal chants resisted every attempt at suppression.

51
00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:13.440
[George] While Sunday afternoons in Congo Square were public celebrations, the supreme spiritual ceremonies of the Voodoo faith occurred in absolute secrecy on St.

52
00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:16.480
[George] John's Eve, the night of June 23.

53
00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:21.360
[George] Along the swampy, cypress-fringed shores of Lake Ponchartrain and Bayou St.

54
00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:26.640
[George] John, thousands of devotees gathered under the summer moon around roaring bonfires.

55
00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:42.720
[George] Dressed in immaculate white cotton, participants sang sacred chants, bathed in the healing waters of the lake, and danced in ecstatic trance states, invoking the serpent spirit Li Grand Zombai to possess their bodies and deliver prophecies of healing and liberation.

56
00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:49.680
[George] Flanked by her giant pet royal python, Marie Lévaux conducted the ceremonies with absolute authority.

57
00:08:49.680 --> 00:09:06.120
[George] Wealthy white businessmen, politicians, and police marshals stood quietly in the shadows of the palm meadows, watching in awe as Marie mediated between the physical world and the spirit realm, seeking her blessings for business contracts, political elections, and romantic fortunes.

58
00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:10.280
[George] Thousands of followers from across Louisiana gathered at Bayou St.

59
00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:23.720
[George] John under the summer moon, burning herbal fires and bathing in the warm brackish waters while Marie Lévau led soaring incantations that captivated socialites, riverboat captains, and enslaved field hands alike.

60
00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:38.040
[George] The annual summer solstice ritual at Lake Pontchartrain drew thousands of seekers who threw offerings of white flowers, silver coins and rum into the waters, praying for physical healing, good fortune, and family reconciliation.

61
00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:44.760
[George] Beyond her spiritual authority, Marie Lévau was a woman of extraordinary, fearless Christian charity.

62
00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:59.000
[George] During the catastrophic yellow fever and cholera epidemics that routinely decimated New Orleans in the 19th century, when wealthy citizens and doctors fled the city in terror, Marie walked fearlessly through the pestilence-ridden wards.

63
00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:10.360
[George] Armed with traditional African herbal medicines, willow bark infusions, and cold vinegar baths, she nursed thousands of fever victims back to health, refusing all payment.

64
00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:15.400
[George] Furthermore, Marie was an angel of mercy to the condemned inmates of the parish prison.

65
00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:28.680
[George] She visited death row prisoners in their damp cells, praying with them, washing their wounds, feeding them home-cooked meals, and erecting small Catholic altars in the dungeon to bring peace to men condemned to the gallows.

66
00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:39.400
[George] In one celebrated case, she successfully secured an executive pardon for two young brothers convicted of murder, earning the permanent gratitude of families across the city.

67
00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:54.960
[George] Marie was renowned throughout New Orleans for her tireless humanitarian work during yellow fever epidemics, fearlessly nursing dying patients of all races with native herbal teas and administering spiritual comfort to condemned prisoners in the parish prison.

68
00:10:54.960 --> 00:11:07.520
[George] Marie visited condemned prisoners in their damp cells during their final hours, preparing their favorite meals, praying with them in French and Latin, and escorting them with gentle dignity to the execution gallows.

69
00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:10.720
[George] Marie Lévau passed away peacefully in her cottage on St.

70
00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:16.320
[George] Anne Street on June 15, 1881, at the advanced age of 79.

71
00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:26.720
[George] The New Orleans Times Picayoon published a glowing, front-page obituary, praising her as a woman of profound piety, boundless charity, and historic grace.

72
00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:30.000
[George] She was entombed in the Glappian family crypt inside St.

73
00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:31.440
[George] Louis Cemetery No.

74
00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:35.840
[George] 1, the oldest existing above-ground cemetery in New Orleans.

75
00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:43.520
[George] Today, more than a century and a half after her passing, Marie Lévaux remains a towering cultural icon of the Crescent City.

76
00:11:43.520 --> 00:12:01.360
[George] Thousands of visitors from around the world visit her whitewashed brick tomb, leaving flowers, coins and candles to honor the memory of a free woman of color who dismantled the barriers of race, gender, and social class to build an immortal spiritual kingdom in the heart of the American South.

77
00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:09.680
[George] Today, visitors from around the world leave offerings of flowers, candles and copper coins at Marie Lévau's whitewashed brick tomb in St.

78
00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:11.120
[George] Louis Cemetery No.

79
00:12:11.120 --> 00:12:17.920
[George] 1, honoring the enduring spiritual power and compassion of New Orleans' most legendary historical icon.

80
00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:30.000
[George] Her enduring legend continues to captivate the imagination of New Orleans, where her name remains synonymous with mystical wisdom, female empowerment, and the unique cultural alchemy of the Crescent City.

81
00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:43.000
[George] Generations of New Orleans residents continue to recount tales of Marie Levau's extraordinary compassion, remembering a woman who broke through racial and social boundaries to bring healing to the afflicted.

82
00:12:43.320 --> 00:12:51.240
[Rebecca] Marie Lévaugh's life reminds us of the power of spirit, intellect, and compassion to transcend the harshest social barriers.

